# Longer explanations on the services and immersive audio pages: what each part of production involves.
DETAILS = {
'services': ('How each part of production works', [
    ('Narration', 'A narrator performs the text aloud with a steady pace, clear diction and consistent character voices. We cast a voice that suits the book and its listeners, then direct the performance so it serves the story rather than the narrator.'),
    ('Recording', 'Recording captures that performance cleanly. A quiet, controlled space and consistent microphone technique keep the voice close and natural from the first chapter to the last, so the listener never notices a change of room or day.'),
    ('Editing', 'Editing removes mistakes, retakes, mouth noises and overlong pauses, and checks the recording against the manuscript line by line so that nothing is skipped, repeated or misread.'),
    ('Mastering', 'Mastering balances the loudness and tone of every chapter so the audiobook plays at an even level from start to finish, and prepares the files to the technical requirements of the platforms the book will be published on.'),
    ('Sound design and music', 'Ambience, Foley and effects place the listener inside a scene, and original music can mark themes, characters and transitions. In an enhanced or immersive production every sound is chosen to make a scene clearer, never to distract from the words.'),
    ('Dramatisation', 'A dramatised audiobook casts different actors for different characters and directs scenes like an audio drama, with sound design and music around them. It suits dialogue-rich fiction, children’s stories and books written to be performed.'),
    ('Translation and other languages', 'We can translate your book into other languages and cast a voice for each edition, so the performance sounds natural to the listeners it is made for rather than like a word-for-word reading.'),
    ('Publishing and delivery', 'Your finished audiobook can be published on MyAudioShelf, where listeners play a free preview and buy without creating an account. We also prepare the agreed final audio, chapter by chapter, with supporting materials for wider distribution.'),
]),
'immersive-audio': ('Immersive audio, explained', [
    ('What makes an audiobook immersive?', 'A classic audiobook is one voice reading the text. An immersive audiobook keeps that performance at the centre and builds the world of the story around it: the room a scene takes place in, the weather outside, the music that carries a memory. You hear where the characters are, not only what they say.'),
    ('Is it the same as spatial audio?', 'No. Spatial or 3D audio is a playback technology that places sounds around your head. An immersive audiobook is a way of producing the story. The two can work together, but an immersive production is made to sound right on ordinary headphones, earbuds and speakers.'),
    ('Which books suit it?', 'Stories with strong settings, children’s audio, drama and dialogue-rich fiction gain the most. Reflective non-fiction and poetry often work best as a classic reading, perhaps with music between chapters. We recommend the treatment that serves each book.'),
    ('Does sound get in the way of the words?', 'It should not. Effects sit under and between lines rather than over them, and the mix keeps the voice clear throughout. If a sound does not help you understand a scene, it does not belong in it.'),
]),
}
